WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:09.423
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Monday, November 9.

2
00:00:09.423 --> 00:00:25.671
Three things today: North Korea's envoy meets Beijing's top brass ahead of APEC, Ukrainian President Zelensky names North Korean missiles in a strike on Ukrainian soil, and South Korea fines Coupang 220 million dollars for market abuse.

3
00:00:25.671 --> 00:00:37.651
That first story matters because two back-to-back high-level contacts between Pyongyang and Beijing have analysts asking whether Kim Jong Un himself may be preparing to move.

4
00:00:37.651 --> 00:00:46.120
Tomorrow, an IHO Infrastructure Center opens in Busan, and a Korean Peninsula peace tourism seminar gets underway in Seoul.

5
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.304
First, the Beijing meeting that has everyone watching Shenzhen.

6
00:00:51.304 --> 00:01:01.233
Kim Song Nam, the Workers' Party international secretary and Pyongyang's top liaison to China, returned home on November 7 after a five-day visit to Beijing.

7
00:01:01.233 --> 00:01:13.059
He met Politburo Standing Committee member Cai Qi and Foreign Minister Wang Yi. That's a significant level of access for an envoy's working visit. The timing is what has analysts talking.

8
00:01:13.059 --> 00:01:22.988
Kim Song Nam's trip came just ten days before U.S. President Donald Trump is due in Shenzhen for the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation summit on November 18.

9
00:01:22.988 --> 00:01:35.194
It also followed Kim Jong Un's own unannounced day trip to Dandong on October 24. Analysts cited by Dong-A Ilbo suggest Kim Song Nam may have relayed messages toward Washington through Beijing.

10
00:01:35.194 --> 00:01:42.909
The back-to-back contacts have fueled speculation about a preparatory visit by Kim Jong Un and a potential summit with Xi.

11
00:01:42.909 --> 00:01:57.581
For Korea policy watchers, the question is whether Pyongyang is using Beijing as a channel to signal interest in engagement ahead of APEC, or simply coordinating with its closest patron before a major moment in U.S.-China diplomacy.

12
00:01:57.581 --> 00:02:06.245
What to watch: whether Kim Jong Un travels in the days before November 18, and what, if anything, emerges from Trump's Shenzhen meetings.

13
00:02:06.245 --> 00:02:12.000
The bottom line: two high-level Pyongyang-Beijing contacts, with the APEC summit days away.

14
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:26.875
Second, North Korean missiles in Ukraine. Previous Ukrainian statements had referred only to "North Korean ballistic missiles" without specifying the type. Ukraine's military is separately investigating the strike.

15
00:02:26.875 --> 00:02:38.504
This attribution matters for anyone tracking North Korean arms transfers. It moves the evidence from generic to specific: a named missile system, a named target, a named consequence.

16
00:02:38.504 --> 00:02:46.683
Zelensky is now on record calling for stronger sanctions directly tied to this strike. Meanwhile, the Democratic-controlled U.S.

17
00:02:46.683 --> 00:02:55.182
Congress is separately pushing legislation that would advance roughly 20 trillion won in Ukraine aid and tighten sanctions on Russia.

18
00:02:55.182 --> 00:03:03.681
That legislative push reflects Democratic majority priorities that diverge from the Republican White House's posture on the conflict.

19
00:03:03.681 --> 00:03:15.630
For Seoul, that divergence is part of a broader challenge: Korea JoongAng Daily reports South Korea plans to commit 100 million dollars in Ukraine aid despite ongoing diplomatic tensions.

20
00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:28.735
Third, South Korea's biggest food delivery fine. South Korea's Fair Trade Commission fined Coupang 220 million dollars for abusing its dominant position in the food delivery market.

21
00:03:28.735 --> 00:03:47.218
SBS reported separately that the total combined fines against Coupang Eats and its rival Baemin reached approximately 630 billion won, which is about 463 million dollars, for pressuring merchants to sell at lower prices, limiting price competition, and raising costs for consumers.

22
00:03:47.218 --> 00:03:54.321
Regulators confirmed the Coupang penalty. It is the largest such fine in South Korea's food delivery sector.

23
00:03:54.321 --> 00:04:05.634
The case turns on how dominant platforms use their market position to squeeze the merchants who depend on them, and ultimately what that means for the prices consumers pay.

24
00:04:05.634 --> 00:04:16.290
For anyone watching South Korea's digital economy regulation, this is a signal that the Fair Trade Commission is willing to move against major platforms at scale.

25
00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:21.420
Coupang, 220 million dollars, the country's largest food delivery market fine.

26
00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:29.291
Now to Pyongyang. State media today led with the agricultural workers' congress and domestic welfare content.

27
00:04:29.291 --> 00:04:37.675
There are no top articles in the KCNA file this morning, and Kim Jong Un has not appeared publicly today, though he was seen one day ago.

28
00:04:37.675 --> 00:04:47.712
Notably, all five recent weapons tests remain absent from the North Korean domestic news agenda. State media is not telling its own population about those launches.

29
00:04:47.712 --> 00:04:59.830
That gap between what Pyongyang does and what it tells its citizens it has done is itself a data point for analysts watching how the leadership manages internal messaging around its weapons program.

30
00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:07.118
The rest of the news, quickly. On the alliance: Democratic Party lawmaker Jin Sung-joon says U.S.

31
00:05:07.118 --> 00:05:18.353
Indo-Pacific Command reaffirmed to him during a Hawaii visit that the conditions-based agreement to transfer wartime operational control to South Korea remains unchanged. The U.S.

32
00:05:18.353 --> 00:05:25.320
side acknowledged that policy-related factors may come into play, but held to the existing bilateral framework.

33
00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:45.593
Defense Minister Kang Shin-chul told the National Assembly that expanding the Cheonghae anti-piracy unit's operational zone from the Somalia coast to south of Oman, adding mine-sweeping vehicles and a P-8A Poseidon aircraft, prepares for threats that, in his words, "cannot be entirely ruled out" near the Strait of Hormuz.

34
00:05:45.593 --> 00:05:57.769
Hours later, Iran's foreign ministry warned Seoul that joining what Tehran called a U.S. military campaign against Iran would damage over 60 years of bilateral relations. Hanwha Aerospace's U.S.

35
00:05:57.769 --> 00:06:10.385
subsidiary plans two test-firings of its Chunmoo multiple rocket launcher system from a vessel off the southern U.S. coast later this month, testing the land-based system for naval deployment. The U.S.

36
00:06:10.385 --> 00:06:15.281
Treasury Department's Financial Crimes Enforcement Network has prohibited U.S.

37
00:06:15.281 --> 00:06:27.834
financial institutions from processing transfers to a Seychelles-based cryptocurrency exchange linked to at least 16.7 billion dollars in illicit transactions, including North Korean money laundering.

38
00:06:27.834 --> 00:06:34.549
On the economy: Samsung is on track to become South Korea's first company to hold 200 trillion won in cash.

39
00:06:34.549 --> 00:06:44.215
Samsung and Hyundai Motor Group formalized a strategic partnership in future mobility and physical AI at Samsung Electronics' Yongin campus on November 9.

40
00:06:44.215 --> 00:06:56.015
South Korea's four major banks face rising non-performing loans in small and medium enterprise lending, with the SME delinquency rate running at roughly double that for large corporations.

41
00:06:56.015 --> 00:07:07.564
The government's Digital Asset Act bill is expected to be released publicly this month, with the major shareholder stake cap in cryptocurrency exchanges likely set at 15 to 20 percent.

42
00:07:07.564 --> 00:07:19.740
The government says remitting funds for the 10 billion dollar U.S. nuclear reactor project under the broader investment pledge requires a full feasibility review before money can be transferred.

43
00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:28.819
An Nvidia controversy has thrown a planned high-profile Korea event into disarray, with the CEO's daughter canceling a planned visit.

44
00:07:28.819 --> 00:07:40.551
South Korean chipmakers Samsung Electronics and SK Hynix could face tighter export compliance requirements if U.S. authorities increase scrutiny of Nvidia's AI chip shipments to China.

45
00:07:40.551 --> 00:07:49.604
In domestic politics: the special counsel bill that passed the National Assembly on November 6 will not go to the November 10 cabinet meeting.

46
00:07:49.604 --> 00:07:57.509
The Blue House says it will be placed on the agenda the following week, with a cabinet vote now expected around November 17.

47
00:07:57.509 --> 00:08:11.918
Justice minister nominee Lee Yong-cheol declined Monday to say whether he would order charges against President Lee Jae-myung dropped, saying he would focus "exclusively on truth-finding in accordance with law and principles."

48
00:08:11.918 --> 00:08:21.864
An election special counsel raided ballot machine firms, finding the National Election Commission spent roughly 17.5 billion won over a decade on two firms.

49
00:08:21.864 --> 00:08:30.280
Opposition lawmakers attacked the government's Future Response Fund in the Policy Committee, calling it a "Future Abandonment Fund."

50
00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:42.138
In polling: Gallup Korea, surveying October 27 to 29, found presidential approval at 39 percent. The Democratic Party stood at 35 percent, down. The People Power Party at 30 percent, up.

51
00:08:42.138 --> 00:08:52.403
A separate Gallup release on November 8 found that 57 percent of Koreans said they do not trust the police, and 58 percent expressed distrust in the prosecution.

52
00:08:52.403 --> 00:09:00.883
South Korea's trade bureau chief met Hungary's deputy prime minister to discuss difficulties facing Samsung SDI and SK On in Hungary.

53
00:09:00.883 --> 00:09:13.315
Foreign Minister Cho Hyun extended condolences over a mass shooting in Poland, noting he had met Polish Foreign Minister Sikorski on the sidelines of the G20 Foreign Ministers Meeting in Atlanta.

54
00:09:13.315 --> 00:09:23.516
The science and technology minister has arrived in Doha for the ITU Plenipotentiary Conference, where South Korea is seeking its tenth consecutive council seat.

55
00:09:23.516 --> 00:09:43.790
Both the Korea Herald and the Chosun Ilbo argue today that the Democratic midterm sweep leaves Seoul with no easy path: a Democratic Congress skeptical of executive-led trade deals, and a Republican White House pressing on cost-sharing and investment commitments simultaneously, require separate engagement strategies.

56
00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:45.977
On Capitol Hill.

57
00:09:45.977 --> 00:09:59.483
The Democratic-controlled Congress is moving to advance roughly 20 trillion won in Ukraine aid and tighten sanctions on Russia, measures that reflect priorities diverging from the Republican White House on the conflict.

58
00:09:59.483 --> 00:10:12.990
Korean-language analysis says Seoul now faces the challenge of engaging a Democratic Congress and a Republican administration with separate and sometimes conflicting asks on alliance cost-sharing, trade, and investment.

59
00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:21.903
Beyond the region. South Korea plans to commit 100 million dollars in Ukraine aid despite ongoing diplomatic tensions.

60
00:10:21.903 --> 00:10:32.230
South Korean companies operating in Vietnam are maintaining their presence despite rising labor and operational costs, the Vietnam Investment Review reported.

61
00:10:32.230 --> 00:10:39.420
Vietnam remains South Korea's largest manufacturing base in Southeast Asia for electronics and consumer goods.

62
00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:53.765
Finally, the markets. Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, closed at about 5,019, down about 0.8 percent. The won strengthened slightly, trading at about 1,346 to the dollar.

63
00:10:53.765 --> 00:11:09.855
Brent crude fell sharply, down about 3.7 percent to just under 95 dollars a barrel. The Bank of Korea's policy rate stands at 3.00 percent, last set in October. That's the Korea Daily Brief for Monday, November 9.

64
00:11:09.855 --> 00:11:14.690
The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
